@node Authentication
@section Authentication

@findex spofy-authenticate

The command @code{spofy-authenticate} initiates the Spotify OAuth2 authorization flow.  It starts a temporary HTTP server on @code{spofy-redirect-port}, opens the user's default browser to Spotify's authorization page, and waits for the redirect callback.  The server silently ignores non-OAuth requests (such as the browser's @samp{favicon.ico} request) and only processes callbacks that carry the expected query parameters.  Once the user grants access, Spofy exchanges the authorization code for access and refresh tokens, stores them along with the expiry time in @code{spofy-token-file}, and shuts down the temporary server. @*

After successful authentication, Spofy runs @code{spofy-authenticated-hook}.  You only need to authenticate once; subsequent sessions reuse the stored refresh token to obtain new access tokens automatically. @*

Any Spofy command that requires an active session will prompt for authentication if no valid tokens are found, so you do not need to call @code{spofy-authenticate} or @code{spofy} explicitly before using other entry points such as the transient menu. @*

@node Dashboard and global control
@section Dashboard and global control

@findex spofy
@findex spofy-dashboard-refresh
@findex spofy-stop

The command @code{spofy} is the main entry point.  It ensures that the user is authenticated (prompting if not), starts player state polling, and opens the @samp{*Spofy*} dashboard buffer.  The dashboard always shows a @strong{now playing} section at the top (track name, artist, and album with release year each on its own line, then a progress bar, then shuffle/repeat state on the left with elapsed/total timestamps right-aligned to the progress bar).  Below it, a configurable list of sections is displayed according to @code{spofy-dashboard-sections} (@ref{Global key and mode-line activation, , Global options}).  By default, the dashboard shows recently played tracks and user playlists.  Each section includes a ``View all'' link that opens the full list in a tabulated-list buffer.  Use the transient popup (@ref{Transient popup}) to discover available keybindings. @*

The now-playing section refreshes automatically when the player state changes.  A 1-second timer interpolates the progress bar between polls so it animates smoothly.  The recently-played section refreshes automatically when the current track changes, so it always reflects the latest listening history without requiring a manual refresh.  The command @code{spofy-dashboard-refresh} forces a full refresh of the entire dashboard. @*

The command @code{spofy-stop} stops player state polling and disables @code{spofy-global-mode}.  Use it when you are done with Spofy and want to stop all background activity. @*

The dashboard keymap provides these bindings: @*

@multitable {aaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Command
@item @samp{SPC}
@tab @code{spofy-play-pause}
@item @samp{n}
@tab @code{spofy-next}
@item @samp{p}
@tab @code{spofy-previous}
@item @samp{/}
@tab @code{spofy-menu}
@item @samp{g}
@tab @code{spofy-dashboard-refresh}
@item @samp{q}
@tab @code{quit-window}
@end multitable

@node Transient popup
@section Transient popup

@findex spofy-menu

The command @code{spofy-menu} opens a transient popup that provides quick access to all Spofy commands from any buffer.  Before showing the popup, it ensures that @code{spofy-global-mode} is active (@ref{Global minor mode}), so invoking @code{spofy-menu} for the first time in a session automatically starts polling, the mode-line display, and the tab-bar display as configured.  The popup header shows the currently playing track.  When @code{spofy-global-mode} is active, this command is bound to @code{spofy-global-key} (default @samp{C-c s}). @*

The popup is organized into three columns: @*

@itemize
@item
@strong{Column 1 — Playback, Volume, Mode:} play/pause, next, previous, seek forward, seek backward, seek to (@samp{t}); volume up/down and set; shuffle and repeat toggles. @*
@item
@strong{Column 2 — Search and Browse:} Spotify search for tracks (@samp{s t}), albums (@samp{s l}), playlists (@samp{s p}), artists (@samp{s a}); library search for saved tracks (@samp{l t}), saved albums (@samp{l a}), playlists (@samp{l p}), context (@samp{l c}); browse saved tracks (@samp{b t}), saved albums (@samp{b a}), playlists (@samp{b p}), current context (@samp{b c}). @*
@item
@strong{Column 3 — Other:} dashboard (@samp{/}), devices (@samp{d}), Wikipedia lookup (@samp{w}), quit (@samp{q}). @*
@end itemize

Volume up/down, seek forward/backward, shuffle, and repeat all keep the menu open so they can be used repeatedly. @*

@node Global minor mode
@section Global minor mode

@findex spofy-global-mode

The command @code{spofy-global-mode} toggles a global minor mode.  When enabled, it: @*

@itemize
@item
Binds @code{spofy-global-key} to @code{spofy-menu}. @*
@item
Starts player state polling. @*
@item
Enables the tab-bar display (if @code{spofy-enable-tab-bar} is non-nil), or the mode-line display (if @code{spofy-enable-mode-line} is non-nil).  Tab-bar takes precedence; the two are mutually exclusive (@ref{Global key and mode-line activation, , Global options}). @*
@end itemize

When disabled, it reverses all of these effects.  Both @code{spofy} and @code{spofy-menu} enable this mode automatically when they are invoked. @*

In addition, Spofy auto-enables the mode at package load time when a display feature (@code{spofy-enable-mode-line} or @code{spofy-enable-tab-bar}) is configured and a non-expired access token is found on disk.  This check is entirely passive: it reads the token file but never makes network requests (no token refresh, no API calls).  If the stored access token has expired, auto-start is silently skipped and the user must invoke @code{spofy-menu} or @code{spofy} to start the mode.  Loading the package eagerly (e.g. with @samp{:demand t} in a @code{use-package} form) is sufficient for the tab-bar or mode-line display to appear at Emacs startup, provided the token has not yet expired. @*

@node Playback control
@section Playback control

@findex spofy-play-pause
@findex spofy-next
@findex spofy-previous
@findex spofy-seek-forward
@findex spofy-seek-backward
@findex spofy-volume-up
@findex spofy-volume-down
@findex spofy-volume-set
@findex spofy-toggle-shuffle
@findex spofy-toggle-repeat
@findex spofy-seek-to
@findex spofy-play-track
@findex spofy-play-context

The command @code{spofy-play-pause} toggles playback between playing and paused.  It checks the current state and sends the appropriate API call.  On success, the local player state is updated immediately so that the mode line and tab bar reflect the change without waiting for the next poll cycle.  When pausing, the interpolated progress is snapshotted so the displayed position does not jump backwards.  When resuming, the interpolation anchor is reset and Spofy issues an immediate re-poll, which picks up any track change that Spotify may have made (for example, advancing to the next track in the queue). @*

The commands @code{spofy-next} and @code{spofy-previous} skip to the next or previous track, respectively.  Both commands re-poll after a short delay on success so the UI reflects the new track without waiting for the next poll cycle.  If the user was playing when they skipped, the playing state is preserved through Spotify's brief transitional pause. @*

The commands @code{spofy-seek-forward} and @code{spofy-seek-backward} move the playback position by @code{spofy-seek-seconds} seconds (@ref{Playback, , Playback options}).  The command @code{spofy-seek-to} prompts for an arbitrary timestamp and jumps to that position.  It accepts plain seconds (@samp{"90"}), @samp{M:SS} (@samp{"1:30"}), or @samp{H:MM:SS} (@samp{"1:05:30"}) format.  The prompt shows the track duration as a guide; the value is clamped to the range [0, duration]. @*

The commands @code{spofy-volume-up} and @code{spofy-volume-down} adjust the volume by @code{spofy-volume-step} percent.  The command @code{spofy-volume-set} prompts for an exact volume level between 0 and 100.  All three commands update the local player state optimistically so that repeated keypresses accumulate without waiting for the API response.  The optimistic value is protected from being overwritten by the next poll cycle, so there is no regression even if Spotify has not yet processed the change. @*

The command @code{spofy-toggle-shuffle} toggles shuffle mode on or off.  The command @code{spofy-toggle-repeat} cycles through three repeat states: off, context (repeat the current album/playlist), and track (repeat the current track).  Both commands update the local player state immediately on success, so the mode line, tab bar, and transient menu reflect the new state without waiting for the next poll.  In the transient popup (@ref{Transient popup}), the current state is shown with the @code{transient-value} face, and both suffixes remain transient so that the user can toggle repeatedly without reopening the menu. @*

The command @code{spofy-play-track} plays a specific track by its Spotify URI@.  If an optional context URI is provided (an album or playlist URI), playback starts within that context at the specified track.  The command @code{spofy-play-context} plays an entire context (album, playlist, or artist) from the beginning.  Both commands are used internally by the browse and search interfaces. @*

@node Polling
@section Polling

@findex spofy-player-start-polling
@findex spofy-player-stop-polling

The command @code{spofy-player-start-polling} starts a timer that polls the Spotify player state at @code{spofy-poll-interval} second intervals.  Polling is necessary because the Spotify Web API does not support push notifications.  When the player state changes, Spofy runs @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook}.  When the track changes specifically, it also runs @code{spofy-player-track-changed-hook}.  When the API reports no active player session (e.g. playback ended or the device went idle), the response may contain null fields such as @code{item}; Spofy normalizes these to @code{nil}, clears the player state, and runs @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook} so that the mode line and tab bar reflect that nothing is playing.  Polling errors are caught unconditionally (even when @code{debug-on-error} is non-nil) to prevent them from stalling the repeat timer. @*

The command @code{spofy-player-stop-polling} cancels the polling timer.  Polling is started automatically by the @code{spofy} command and @code{spofy-global-mode}, and stopped by @code{spofy-stop}. @*

@node Device management
@section Device management

@findex spofy-select-device

The Spotify Web API can control playback on any device logged into the user's account: desktop apps, mobile phones, web players, smart speakers, and more.  The command @code{spofy-select-device} fetches the list of available devices, presents them in a @code{completing-read} prompt, and transfers playback to the selected device. @*

Any playback command that requires an active device will automatically call @code{spofy-select-device} if none is found, so you never need to select a device manually before issuing a command. @*

@node Search
