@findex spofy-player-current-track
@findex spofy-player-playing-p
@findex spofy-player-current-track-id
@findex spofy-player-interpolated-progress

The function @code{spofy-player-current-track} returns the current track as a cons cell @code{(NAME . ARTIST)}, or @code{nil} if nothing is playing.  This is useful in custom mode-line constructs or hook functions. @*

The function @code{spofy-player-playing-p} returns non-nil if Spotify is currently playing. @*

The function @code{spofy-player-current-track-id} returns the Spotify ID of the currently playing track, or @code{nil}.  This is used internally by search and browse buffers to highlight the now-playing row. @*

The function @code{spofy-player-interpolated-progress} returns the estimated current playback position in milliseconds.  When the track is playing, it adds the wall-clock time elapsed since the last poll to the last-known progress, clamping at the track duration.  When paused, it returns the stored progress directly.  The dashboard progress bar and tab-bar @samp{%G} specifier use this function to update smoothly between poll cycles. @*

@node Entity access in buffers
@section Entity access in buffers

@findex spofy-ui-entity-at-point

The function @code{spofy-ui-entity-at-point} returns the full Spotify API alist for the entity on the current @code{tabulated-list} row in any Spofy buffer.  It works uniformly across browse, search, library, and playlist buffers by looking up the row ID in a shared buffer-local entity table managed by @samp{spofy-ui.el}.  This is useful for writing custom actions or hook functions that need to access entity metadata. @*

@node API client
@section API client

@findex spofy-api-get
@findex spofy-api-get-sync
@findex spofy-api-put
@findex spofy-api-post
@findex spofy-api-delete

The API client in @samp{spofy-api.el} provides five high-level functions for interacting with the Spotify Web API: @*

@itemize
@item
@code{spofy-api-get} sends an asynchronous GET request to an endpoint with optional query parameters. @*
@item
@code{spofy-api-get-sync} sends a synchronous GET request and returns the parsed JSON response directly, or @code{nil} on error.  It is used by the consult integration (@ref{Consult integration}) and other code paths that require a return value rather than a callback. @*
@item
@code{spofy-api-put} sends a PUT request with an optional JSON body. @*
@item
@code{spofy-api-post} sends a POST request with an optional JSON body. @*
@item
@code{spofy-api-delete} sends a DELETE request with an optional JSON body. @*
@end itemize

All five accept an endpoint path (relative to @samp{https://api.spotify.com/v1/}), optional data, and---for the asynchronous variants---a callback function that receives the parsed JSON response.  They handle authentication headers, token refresh, rate limiting (429 responses), transient error retry, and response buffer cleanup automatically.  If no valid access token is available, the request is skipped and a message suggesting @samp{M-x spofy-authenticate} is shown instead of sending a malformed request. @*

These functions are the building blocks for all of Spofy's features.  Advanced users who want to extend Spofy with custom API calls can use them directly. @*

@node Authentication helpers
@section Authentication helpers

@findex spofy-auth-access-token
@findex spofy-auth-refresh-token

The function @code{spofy-auth-access-token} returns the current OAuth2 access token, or @code{nil} if unavailable or expired.  Because the token expiry is persisted to disk alongside the tokens, this function can determine after an Emacs restart whether the stored access token is still valid without contacting Spotify.  If the token has expired and a refresh token is available, it automatically attempts a refresh before returning @code{nil}. @*

The function @code{spofy-auth-refresh-token} explicitly uses the stored refresh token to obtain a new access token from Spotify.  The request is synchronous with a 10-second timeout to prevent Emacs from blocking indefinitely if the Spotify token endpoint is unreachable.  It requires @code{spofy-client-id} and @code{spofy-client-secret} to be set, and signals an error if either is missing.  This is called automatically when needed, but can be invoked manually if you encounter authentication issues. @*

@node Formatting utilities
@section Formatting utilities

@findex spofy-ui-format-duration-ms
@findex spofy-ui-format-artists
@findex spofy-ui-truncate
@findex spofy-ui-progress-bar-only
@findex spofy-ui-progress-time
@findex spofy-ui-insert-header
@findex spofy-ui-play-pause-icon
@findex spofy-ui-shuffle-indicator
@findex spofy-ui-repeat-indicator
@findex spofy-ui--after-tabulated-list-print

The function @code{spofy-ui-format-duration-ms} takes a duration in milliseconds and returns a human-readable @code{"M:SS"} string. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-format-artists} takes a list of artist alists (as returned by the Spotify API) and formats them as a comma-separated string of names. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-truncate} truncates a string to a given maximum display width.  It accepts an optional FACE argument; when provided, the face is applied to the result and, if truncation occurred, the last three characters are marked with the @code{spofy-fade} text property (levels 1, 2, 3).  After @code{tabulated-list-print}, the advice function @code{spofy-ui--after-tabulated-list-print} calls @code{spofy-ui--apply-buffer-fades}, which creates overlays on the marked characters with foreground colors blended toward the default background at 25%, 50%, and 75% respectively.  This produces a smooth fade-out effect instead of a hard cut or an explicit ellipsis character.  Because the color blending happens at display time via overlays rather than being baked into text properties, the fade automatically adapts when the user switches themes: @code{spofy-ui--refresh-fades} is registered on @code{enable-theme-functions} and recomputes all fade overlays in Spofy buffers.  It uses @code{truncate-string-to-width} internally, so column alignment is correct even with variable-width characters.  To prevent @code{tabulated-list-mode} from adding its own ellipsis (which in Emacs 30 uses a @code{display} text property that overrides the fade), @code{spofy-ui-set-format} sets @code{truncate-string-ellipsis} to @code{""} buffer-locally.  All entry formatting functions in the browse, library, search, consult, and playlist modules pass the appropriate face to @code{spofy-ui-truncate}, which handles both truncation and propertizing in one call.  The mode-line and tab-bar modules call it without a face, so truncation there is silent. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-progress-bar-only} builds a text progress bar for a given progress and duration (both in milliseconds) at a given character width.  It uses full-block characters (U+2588) for the filled portion with the @code{spofy-progress-filled} face, and light-shade characters (U+2591) for the empty portion with @code{spofy-progress-empty}.  The function @code{spofy-ui-progress-time} returns a time display string (e.g. @code{"1:23 / 3:45"}) for a given progress and duration.  These functions are used by the dashboard and the tab-bar @samp{%G} and @samp{%T} format specifiers respectively. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-insert-header} inserts a list of strings at the top of the current buffer, each on its own line with the @code{spofy-header} face.  It is used by the album, artist, and playlist browse views to render header metadata above the track listing. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-play-pause-icon} returns a play or pause icon string based on the player state alist: @samp{⏸} when playing, @samp{▶} when paused.  The function @code{spofy-ui-shuffle-indicator} returns @samp{⇌} when shuffle is on, or an empty string when off.  The function @code{spofy-ui-repeat-indicator} returns @samp{↻} for context repeat, @samp{↻₁} for track repeat, or an empty string when repeat is off.  These functions are used by the mode-line and tab-bar display modules. @*

