@node Pagination
@subsection Pagination

All Spofy @code{tabulated-list-mode} buffers share a single pagination mechanism.  Results load automatically as the user scrolls: the function @code{spofy-ui--maybe-load-more} is registered on @code{window-scroll-functions} and triggers a fetch when the window end is within a few lines of the buffer end.  A @code{post-command-hook} complement ensures pagination fires reliably under @code{pixel-scroll-precision-mode}, which can miss @code{window-scroll-functions} events.  A guard variable (@code{spofy-ui--loading-more}) prevents duplicate requests while one is already in flight, and the URL is checked with @code{stringp} to avoid attempting a fetch when the JSON @code{:null} sentinel has not yet been normalized.  Each buffer stores a buffer-local handler (set by the module that created the buffer) that knows how to extract items and the next-page URL from the API response.  This keeps all pagination logic in one place in @samp{spofy-ui.el} while letting each module control its own entry formatting.  Both @code{spofy-api--extract-paged-results} and @code{spofy-ui-load-more} normalize JSON @code{:null} values to @code{nil}, so that a @code{null} next-page URL from the Spotify API is correctly treated as the end of results. @*

@node Entity storage and list rendering
@subsection Entity storage and list rendering

@findex spofy-ui-store-entity
@findex spofy-ui-entity-at-point
@findex spofy-ui-extract-id
@findex spofy-ui-album-year
@findex spofy-ui-playing-indicator
@findex spofy-ui-render-list

All Spofy list buffers share a common entity storage mechanism provided by @samp{spofy-ui.el}.  The buffer-local variable @code{spofy-ui--entities} holds a hash table mapping row IDs (typically Spotify URIs) to the full API alists returned by the Spotify Web API@.  The function @code{spofy-ui-store-entity} stores an entity under a given key, lazily creating the hash table on first use.  The function @code{spofy-ui-entity-at-point} retrieves the entity for the current @code{tabulated-list} row by looking up its ID in the hash table.  Modules such as @samp{spofy-playlist.el}, @samp{spofy-browse.el}, @samp{spofy-search.el}, and @samp{spofy-library.el} use these functions instead of maintaining their own per-module entity tables. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-extract-id} extracts the bare Spotify ID from a URI of the form @code{spotify:TYPE:ID}, returning the ID portion.  If the string does not match the URI pattern, it is returned unchanged, so plain IDs can be passed through safely. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-album-year} extracts the four-digit release year from an album alist.  It reads the @code{release_date} field and returns the first four characters if they form a year, or the raw value otherwise.  All album formatting functions in the library, browse, and search modules use this instead of inlining the extraction logic. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-playing-indicator} returns a now-playing indicator string for a given track URI@.  It compares the URI against the currently playing track ID (via @code{spofy-player-current-track-id}) and returns a play icon (@samp{▶}) propertized with the @code{spofy-playing-icon} face when they match, or an empty string otherwise.  Track formatting functions in library, browse, and search buffers call this to mark the active row. @*

The function @code{spofy-ui-render-list} provides the standard recipe for setting up a Spofy @code{tabulated-list-mode} buffer.  It takes a buffer name, a mode function, a list of entries, a next-page URL, and an optional load-more handler and display function.  It creates or reuses the named buffer, erases it, activates the mode, sets the pagination state and entries, prints the list, and displays the buffer (defaulting to @code{pop-to-buffer}; browse views pass @code{switch-to-buffer} instead).  Modules call this function instead of duplicating the buffer-setup boilerplate. @*

@node Consult integration
@chapter Consult integration

@findex consult-spofy-track
@findex consult-spofy-album
@findex consult-spofy-artist
@findex consult-spofy-playlist
@findex spofy-library-search-tracks
@findex spofy-library-search-albums
@findex spofy-library-search-playlists
@findex consult-spofy-device
@findex spofy-library-search-context

The file @samp{spofy-consult.el} provides @code{consult} completion sources for searching and selecting Spotify entities from the minibuffer.  These require the @samp{consult} package.  The dependency is soft-loaded so that byte-compilation of the main Spofy package succeeds even when @samp{consult} is not in the load path; at runtime, @samp{consult} must be installed for these commands to work. @*

@vindex spofy-consult-columns

All candidates are displayed in aligned tabular columns with faces matching the search result buffers.  The user option @code{spofy-consult-columns} controls the column widths per entity type; see its docstring for the column layout of each type. @*

The command @code{consult-spofy-track} lets you search for tracks with live narrowing as you type (debounced at 300ms to avoid excessive API calls).  Candidates show Name, Artist(s), Album, and Duration columns.  Selecting a track plays it immediately. @*

The command @code{consult-spofy-album} provides the same experience for albums, with Name, Year, Artist(s), and number of tracks columns.  Selecting an album opens the album view (@ref{Album view}). @*

The command @code{consult-spofy-artist} searches artists, with Name, Genres, and Followers columns.  Selecting an artist opens the artist view (@ref{Artist view}). @*

The command @code{consult-spofy-playlist} searches all Spotify playlists with live narrowing, with Name, Owner, and number of tracks columns.  Selecting a playlist opens the playlist detail view (@ref{Playlist detail view}). @*

The following three commands search within the user's own library rather than the full Spotify catalog.  They rely on the library cache (@ref{Cache management}), which is populated asynchronously in the background when Spofy starts.  All saved items are available for instant client-side filtering as you type.  If the cache is not yet ready (e.g., at the start of a session), the command displays a message and starts the background fetch.  These are bound under the @samp{l} prefix in the transient popup (@ref{Transient popup}). @*

The command @code{spofy-library-search-tracks} searches the user's saved tracks with incremental narrowing.  Candidates show Name, Artist(s), Album, and Duration columns.  Selecting a track plays it immediately. @*

The command @code{spofy-library-search-albums} searches saved albums with incremental narrowing, with Name, Year, Artist(s), and number of tracks columns.  Selecting an album opens the album view (@ref{Album view}). @*

The command @code{spofy-library-search-playlists} searches the user's playlists with incremental narrowing, with Name, Owner, and number of tracks columns.  Selecting a playlist opens the playlist detail view (@ref{Playlist detail view}). @*

The command @code{consult-spofy-device} fetches available playback devices with incremental narrowing, with Name and Type columns.  Selecting a device transfers playback to it. @*

The command @code{spofy-library-search-context} fetches the tracklist of the currently playing album or playlist and lets you jump to any track with incremental narrowing.  It is not available for Spotify-generated contexts such as radio or daily mixes.  This command is bound to @samp{l c} in the transient popup (@ref{Transient popup}). @*

The Spotify search commands use @code{consult--dynamic-collection} for incremental search with synchronous API requests (via @code{url-retrieve-synchronously}), debounced to avoid excessive API calls.  The library search commands pass a pre-fetched candidate list directly to @code{consult--read}, since all items are already in memory from the library cache.  In both cases, each candidate stores the full Spotify entity in a text property; the @code{:lookup} mechanism ensures the original propertized candidate is returned on selection, so the entity data is available for the action.  This makes them compatible with the embark integration (@ref{Embark integration}). @*

@node Embark integration
@chapter Embark integration

@findex spofy-embark-copy-uri

The file @samp{spofy-embark.el} registers four entity categories with @samp{embark}, each with a keymap of contextual actions.  Like @samp{spofy-consult.el}, the @samp{embark} dependency is soft-loaded for byte-compilation compatibility.  These actions work on Spotify entities selected through @code{consult} (@ref{Consult integration}) or in @code{tabulated-list} buffers. @*

The @strong{spofy-track} category provides actions to play the track, add it to a playlist, save it to the library, view its album, view its artist, open its Wikipedia article (@samp{w}), or copy its Spotify URI@. @*

The @strong{spofy-album} category provides actions to play the album, save it, view its tracks, view its artist, open its Wikipedia article (@samp{w}), or copy its URI@. @*

The @strong{spofy-artist} category provides actions to play the artist's top tracks, view the artist's albums, open the artist's Wikipedia article (@samp{w}), or copy the URI@. @*

The @strong{spofy-playlist} category provides actions to play the playlist, view its tracks, follow it, unfollow it, or copy its URI@. @*

The command @code{spofy-embark-copy-uri} copies the Spotify URI of the target entity to the kill ring.  This is available in all four category keymaps and is useful for sharing links or constructing org-mode links manually. @*

In addition to single-target actions, @samp{spofy-embark.el} registers exporters for the @samp{spofy-track}, @samp{spofy-album}, and @samp{spofy-playlist} categories.  When using a consult library search command (e.g., @code{spofy-library-search-tracks}), you can narrow the candidates, then call @code{embark-export} to export the matching candidates into a standard Spofy browse buffer with all the usual keybindings (play, view album, view artist, unsave, add to playlist, etc.).  This is useful when you want to filter your library and then explore or act on the results interactively rather than selecting a single item. @*

@node Org-mode integration
@chapter Org-mode integration

@findex spofy-org-follow
@findex spofy-org-store
@findex spofy-org-export

The file @samp{spofy-org.el} registers a @samp{spotify:} link type with Org mode, supporting three operations.  It is loaded automatically when @code{spofy-global-mode} is enabled. @*

The function @code{spofy-org-follow} handles opening @code{spotify:} links.  It parses the link path to determine the entity type and ID, then dispatches to the appropriate Spofy command: tracks are played, while albums, artists, and playlists open their respective browse views (@ref{Browsing}).  This means you can write @samp{[[spotify:track:4uLU6hMCjMI75M1A2tKUQC][Never Gonna Give You Up]]} in an Org file and open it to play the track. @*

The function @code{spofy-org-store} enables @samp{org-store-link} in any Spofy buffer.  It extracts the URI and a descriptive label from the entity at point and stores it for later insertion with @samp{org-insert-link}. @*

The function @code{spofy-org-export} generates appropriate links for export backends.  In HTML, @LaTeX{}, and Markdown, it produces links to @samp{open.spotify.com/TYPE/ID} so readers can open the content in their browser or Spotify app. @*

@node Wikipedia integration
@chapter Wikipedia integration

@findex spofy-wikipedia
@vindex spofy-wikipedia-language
@vindex spofy-wikipedia-db-file
@vindex spofy-wikipedia-backend
@vindex spofy-wikipedia-model
@vindex spofy-wikipedia-fallback-to-artist

The file @samp{spofy-wikipedia.el} provides a command to open the Wikipedia article for the album or musical work that is currently playing.  It handles non-classical and classical music differently, using two distinct lookup strategies. @*

For non-classical music, Spofy first attempts a precise lookup via Wikidata's SPARQL endpoint, using the Spotify album ID (Wikidata property P2205) to find the corresponding Wikipedia article directly.  This is the most reliable path when the album is registered in Wikidata (~40k albums).  If the Wikidata lookup finds no match, Spofy falls back to searching the Wikipedia API using the album name and artist, then validates that the top result is a genuine album article by checking for the presence of @samp{Template:Infobox album}.  No LLM is involved in this path, so it is fast and deterministic.  When Spotify lists multiple artists (e.g., composer and performer), only the first name is used in Wikipedia searches to avoid failed lookups on combined strings. @*

Artist lookups similarly try Wikidata first (property P1902, ~65k artists) before falling back to Wikipedia search. @*

For classical music, a different approach is necessary because the Spotify album name (e.g., ``Beethoven: Symphonies Nos. 5 & 7'') does not correspond to a single Wikipedia article.  Instead, Spofy uses an LLM (via @samp{gptel}) to extract the underlying musical work from the track and album metadata and return its Wikipedia article title.  For example, a track named ``Symphony No. 5 in C minor, Op. 67: I@. Allegro con brio'' resolves to the Wikipedia article ``Symphony No. 5 (Beethoven)''. @*

Classification of tracks as classical or non-classical relies primarily on the Spotify API's genre tags for the artist.  If any of the artist's genres match a set of classical patterns (such as ``classical'', ``orchestral'', ``opera'', or ``baroque''), the track is treated as classical.  When an artist has no genre tags at all — which is common for lesser-known classical performers — Spofy falls back to heuristic matching on the track name, looking for patterns such as opus numbers (@samp{Op.}), catalog numbers (@samp{BWV}, @samp{K.}, @samp{RV}, etc.), key signatures (@samp{in C minor}), movement indicators (@samp{I.}, @samp{II.}), and tempo markings (@samp{Allegro}, @samp{Adagio}, etc.). @*

All lookup results are cached persistently in a SQLite database (Emacs 29+ built-in support required) so that repeated plays of the same album or track never trigger redundant API or LLM calls.  Artist classifications are also cached. @*

The command @code{spofy-wikipedia} is the main entry point.  It reads the currently playing track from the player state (@ref{Playback control}), classifies the artist, and dispatches to the appropriate lookup path.  When a Wikipedia article is found, it is opened via @code{browse-url}.  The command is also available in the transient popup (@ref{Transient popup}) under @samp{w}. @*

@findex spofy-wikipedia-track
@findex spofy-wikipedia-album
@findex spofy-wikipedia-artist

Wikipedia lookup actions are available through Embark (@ref{Embark integration}) on tracks, albums, and artists (bound to @samp{w} in each keymap).  The function @code{spofy-wikipedia-track} classifies the artist and dispatches to either the album or work lookup.  The function @code{spofy-wikipedia-album} performs a direct album lookup.  The function @code{spofy-wikipedia-artist} looks up the artist's Wikipedia page.  All three receive the Embark target string and extract entity metadata from its text properties. @*

@menu
* Wikipedia user options::
@end menu

@node Wikipedia user options
@section Wikipedia user options

The user option @code{spofy-wikipedia-language} controls which language edition of Wikipedia to use.  The default value is @samp{"en"} for the English Wikipedia.  Set it to another language code (e.g., @samp{"de"}, @samp{"fr"}, @samp{"es"}) to look up articles in that language instead. @*

The user option @code{spofy-wikipedia-db-file} specifies the path to the SQLite database file for caching lookup results.  The default is @samp{spofy-wikipedia.sqlite} in the user Emacs directory. @*

The user option @code{spofy-wikipedia-backend} specifies the @samp{gptel} backend name to use for classical music lookups (e.g., @samp{"Gemini"} or @samp{"Claude"}).  When @code{nil} (the default), the backend is inferred from @code{spofy-wikipedia-model} or falls back to the global @code{gptel-backend}. @*

The user option @code{spofy-wikipedia-model} specifies the @samp{gptel} model for classical music lookups.  When @code{nil} (the default), it uses the global @code{gptel-model}.  These two options follow the same resolution pattern as @samp{elfeed-ai.el}: setting the model alone is sufficient if it uniquely identifies a backend. @*

The user option @code{spofy-wikipedia-fallback-to-artist} controls what happens when no Wikipedia article is found for the album or musical work.  When non-nil (the default), Spofy falls back to opening the artist's Wikipedia page instead.  This is useful because many albums and classical works lack dedicated Wikipedia articles, while their artists or composers typically do.  Set it to @code{nil} to signal an error instead. @*

@node Faces
@chapter Faces

@vindex spofy-track-name
@vindex spofy-artist-name
@vindex spofy-album-name
@vindex spofy-now-playing-track
@vindex spofy-now-playing-artist
@vindex spofy-now-playing-album
@vindex spofy-playing
@vindex spofy-playing-icon
@vindex spofy-header
@vindex spofy-progress-filled
@vindex spofy-progress-empty
@vindex spofy-muted

Spofy defines a set of faces in the @code{spofy-faces} customization group for controlling the appearance of its buffers: @*

@itemize
@item
@code{spofy-track-name} is used for track names in all listings.  It inherits from @code{font-lock-keyword-face}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-artist-name} is used for artist names.  It inherits from @code{font-lock-function-name-face}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-album-name} is used for album names.  It inherits from @code{font-lock-type-face}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-now-playing-track}, @code{spofy-now-playing-artist}, and @code{spofy-now-playing-album} are used in the dashboard now-playing section.  They inherit from the corresponding base faces with a larger height (1.15×). @*
@item
@code{spofy-playing} marks the currently playing track in any listing.  It uses ultra-bold weight, layered on top of each column's own face so that the original colors are preserved.  All columns of the playing row use this face, including the duration column.  The cursor is hidden in all table buffers.  A box border is drawn around the entire playing line using the default foreground color.  Due to an Emacs display limitation, the border's vertical lines may not render after a theme change until the buffer is refreshed with @samp{g}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-playing-icon} is used for the ▶ icon on the currently playing track.  It inherits from @code{spofy-playing}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-header} is used for the header area in album, artist, and playlist detail views.  It inherits from @code{header-line}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-progress-filled} is used for the filled portion of the progress bar.  It inherits from @code{success}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-progress-empty} is used for the empty portion of the progress bar.  It inherits from @code{shadow}. @*
@item
@code{spofy-muted} is used for secondary text like durations and dates.  It inherits from @code{shadow}. @*
@end itemize

All faces can be customized with @samp{M-x customize-group RET spofy-faces RET}. @*

@node Hooks
@chapter Hooks

@vindex spofy-player-state-changed-hook
@vindex spofy-player-track-changed-hook
@vindex spofy-authenticated-hook

The hook @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook} runs whenever any aspect of the player state changes: track, play/pause state, shuffle, repeat, volume, or device.  The mode-line display and dashboard now-playing section both use this hook to stay up to date.  Add your own functions to this hook if you want to react to any state change. @*

The hook @code{spofy-player-track-changed-hook} runs only when the current track changes.  This is a subset of state changes and is useful for notifications or logging.  The dashboard recently-played section hooks into this to refresh automatically when the track changes (@ref{Dashboard and global control}).  All Spofy list buffers (album, playlist, library tracks, search results, top tracks, recently played, and artist top tracks) also hook into this to re-render their entries so the green playing indicator moves to the new track without a manual refresh. @*

The hook @code{spofy-authenticated-hook} runs once after a successful OAuth2 authentication.  Use it to perform setup that depends on having a valid token, such as pre-fetching the user's playlists. @*

@node Mode-line display
@chapter Mode-line display

@findex spofy-mode-line-mode

The command @code{spofy-mode-line-mode} toggles a global minor mode that adds a Spofy segment to the mode line.  The segment shows the currently playing track formatted according to @code{spofy-mode-line-format} (@ref{Mode-line, , Mode-line options}). @*

When enabled, the mode hooks into @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook} to rebuild the mode-line string whenever the player state changes, then calls @code{force-mode-line-update} to refresh all frames. @*

The mode is enabled automatically when @code{spofy-global-mode} is active and @code{spofy-enable-mode-line} is non-nil, provided @code{spofy-enable-tab-bar} is @code{nil} (@ref{Global key and mode-line activation, , Global options}).  When both options are non-nil, tab-bar display takes precedence and mode-line display is not enabled. @*

@node Tab-bar display
@chapter Tab-bar display

@findex spofy-tab-bar-mode

The command @code{spofy-tab-bar-mode} toggles a global minor mode that adds a Spofy segment to the tab bar.  The segment shows the currently playing track formatted according to @code{spofy-tab-bar-format} (@ref{Tab-bar, , Tab-bar options}). @*

When enabled, the mode inserts a @code{tab-bar-format-spofy} entry into @code{tab-bar-format} at a position determined by @code{spofy-tab-bar-alignment} (@ref{Tab-bar, , Tab-bar options}) and hooks into @code{spofy-player-state-changed-hook} to rebuild the tab-bar string whenever the player state changes.  When the format string contains @samp{%g}, a 1-second timer is also started to update the progress bar smoothly between poll cycles.  When disabled, it removes the entry, stops the progress timer, and cleans up any @code{tab-bar-format-align-right} entry that was added automatically. @*

The mode is enabled automatically when @code{spofy-global-mode} is active and @code{spofy-enable-tab-bar} is non-nil (@ref{Global key and mode-line activation, , Global options}).  When enabled, it takes precedence over the mode-line display to avoid showing track information twice. @*

